ns Christian  Andersen's 19th century fairy tale but  the same name have a lot in common well  except for a happy ending of course both  stories start with young gorgeous  mermaids who visit the surface they both  saved the prince from drowning and fall  in love with him wanting nothing more  than to marry their love  the mermaids visit a sea witch to strike  a deal and here's where their stories  start to differ in Ariel's original  story she gives the sea which her voice  and tongue in exchange for human lace  but there's a catch walking on her new  legs is agonizing  if Ariel succeeds in getting the prince  to marry her she'll be granted a human  soul but if she fails her heart will  break  and she'll dissolve into sea foam  unfortunately for the little mermaid the  Prince marries a neighboring princess  who he believes to have saved him from  the shipwreck the mermaids sisters  strike a deal with the sea witch to save  her life  if the mermaid succeeds in murdering the  prince with a special knife and placing  a drop of blood on her feet she will  live understandably the little mermaid  is unable to carry out this task and  dissolves into seafoam princess Belle we  weren't kidding around when we said that  Belle and beasts love story was the real  deal Disney's the Beauty and the Beast  was inspired by real-life events that  occurred in France during the 16th  century I never felt this way about  anyone I want to do something for her  the fairy tale was written by French  author Gabrielle Suzanne Barbeau de  Villanueva she drew inspiration from  Petra's Gonsalves and his bride-to-be  Katherine's very peculiar romance Petra  suffered from a condition called  hypertrichosis which caused him to grow  thick hair all over his body and face it  made him look like a total beast and he  was treated as such by the town spoke  Katherine and Petrus got married and had  seven children and managed to live out  their version of a happily ever after so  there you guys have it 